UID:10001046-1790274600-1790281800@hmh.org
SUMMARY:Unexpected Routes: Refugee Writers in Mexico
DESCRIPTION:Join Holocaust Museum Houston for an evening lecture featuring Dr. Tabea Linhard\, author of Unexpected Routes: Refugee Writers in Mexico. \nUnexpected Routes chronicles the refugee journeys of six writers whose lives were upended by fascism in the aftermath of the Spanish Civil War and during World War II. During her lecture\, Dr. Linhard will explore the stories of German author Ruth Rewald\, who was murdered during the Holocaust\, and Czech writer and journalist Egon Erwin Kisch\, who wrote about the Holocaust while in Mexico. While the writers she discusses in the book came from different backgrounds and wrote in different languages\, they all made sense of their forced displacement in works that reveal their conflicted relationships with the people and places they encountered in transit as well as in Mexico. The literary output of these brilliant\, prolific\, but also flawed individuals reflects the most salient contradictions of what it meant to escape from fascistoccupied Europe. \nThis event is free and open to the public but advanced registration is required.
URL:https://hmh.org/event/unexpected-routes-refugee-writers-in-mexico/
LOCATION:Albert and Ethel Herzstein Theater
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://hmh.org/wp-content/uploads/Unexpected-Routes-.jpg
END:VEVENT
